\DOC LOCAL_ROUGHSQR_P256K1_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_ROUGHSQR_P256K1_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Discharges one inlined "rough squaring" subroutine block in an x86 {eventually}
symbolic-execution goal, advancing the state variable past the block and
recording the mathematical specification of its result.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument is an {int} ({0} in every observed trace) selecting an
offset/instance of the block being handled; the second is a {string list}
naming the block's output and input variables — the first name becomes the
newly introduced result variable, the second is the operand already present in
the assumptions. Applied to a goal whose conclusion is
{eventually x86 (\s'. ...) s_n}, the tactic yields a single subgoal with the
same postcondition {P} applied to a fresh successor state variable {s_{n+1}}:
{
   A ?- eventually x86 P s_n
   =============================================  LOCAL_ROUGHSQR_P256K1_TAC 0 [out; in]
   A' ?- eventually x86 P s_{n+1}
}
\noindent Here {A'} is {A} with all assumptions about the old state —
program-counter value, {bytes_loaded}, register and memory contents, and the
accumulated {MAYCHANGE} relation — removed and re-added rephrased for the new
state, the program counter advanced past the block and the {MAYCHANGE} relation
extended by the destination memory region; purely arithmetic side conditions on
the old variables are kept unchanged. Appended to {A'} are new assumptions
giving the value stored in the destination region and the specification of the
named result variable: a bound on its size and a modular congruence relating it
to the square of the named input.

\COMMENTS
All observed traces show this single-subgoal, state-advancing behaviour on
goals of this one form.

\ENDDOC
